    "body": "### Describe your issue\n\nThe aggregate CI gates can report \"passing\" without the test jobs having succeeded, in two cases.\n\n**1. A needed job is cancelled (e.g. by its timeout).**\n`ci-success`, `ci-early-success` (`ci.yml`) and `sanitizers-success` (`sanitizers.yml`) use\n\n```yaml\nif: ${{ always() && !contains(needs.*.result, 'cancelled') }}\n```\n\nWhen any need is `cancelled`, the gate itself is skipped, and GitHub counts a skipped required check as passing. The `FAIL` expression already treats `cancelled` as failure, but it never runs in that case. A job that exceeds `timeout-minutes` ends as `cancelled`.\n\nLinks (at 7b3aab3633c00a8e05f2e2ff13c044bd91ccb1ed):\nhttps://github.com/cython/cython/blob/7b3aab3633c00a8e05f2e2ff13c044bd91ccb1ed/.github/workflows/ci.yml#L437-L444\nhttps://github.com/cython/cython/blob/7b3aab3633c00a8e05f2e2ff13c044bd91ccb1ed/.github/workflows/sanitizers.yml#L81-L88\n\n**2. `file-changes` fails.**\n`file-changes` is not in the gates' `needs`. If it fails (API error, rate limit), the jobs that depend on it are skipped, and the gates see only skips. Separately, `dorny/paths-filter` in API mode lists at most 3000 files per pull request without detecting the truncation (dorny/paths-filter#227), so very large pull requests can be mis-detected as not touching code.\n\n**Suggested fix**\n- Replace the gate condition with `if: ${{ !cancelled() }}`. In a test repository I checked that after a needed job hits its timeout, `cancelled()` is `false` in the dependent job and `needs.<job>.result` is `cancelled`, so the gate runs and `FAIL` becomes 1. A run cancelled as a whole (for example superseded by a newer push) still skips the gate.\n- Add `file-changes` to the gates' `needs` (its failure then sets `FAIL`).\n\nI can open a PR with these changes if that helps.",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
